{"success": true, "message": "Test details sent successfully", "data": {"test": {"_id": "69de21eb7e39d99b57bc5aaf", "analysisVideoCdn": "yt", "exam": ["605dfcb2f226ac25a8bd5fef"], "ctype": null, "position": 0, "order": 12, "enableCalculator": false, "isContinuousQuestion": false, "reattemptAvailable": false, "singleSectionMultipleSubjects": false, "timeRestricted": false, "title": "Gravitation - Test 3", "description": "Previous Year Questions as Chapter Test", "syllabus": "This test contains questions from all topics of this chapter included in the JEE Main syllabus. It includes a mix of questions from PYQs of JEE Main 2019 - 2025.", "totalTime": 50, "totalQuestions": 25, "totalMarks": 100, "bonusQuestions": null, "bonusMarks": null, "startDate": "2026-04-15T03:30:00.000Z", "endDate": "2027-04-30T00:00:00.000Z", "type": "free", "class": "605df635a37b7025c438d685", "instruction": "608289ffb160b022e9442c83", "subjects": [{"subjectId": "607018ee404ae53194e73d90", "totalMarks": 100, "bonusMarks": 0, "totalQuestions": 25, "bonusQuestions": 0, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a3a0d"}], "sections": [{"sectionId": {"_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a39c7", "title": "Physics Single Correct", "sectionType": "normal", "subject": "607018ee404ae53194e73d90", "questions": [{"_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a395f", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb0"], "topics": ["6117b60a478ae845c43c63f1"], "concepts": ["6117b607478ae845c43c62fb"], "question": {"text": "The mass density of a spherical galaxy varies as&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"italic\">K</mi><mi mathvariant=\"italic\">r</mi></mfrac></math>&nbsp;over a large distance&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>r</mi></math>&nbsp;from its center. In that region, a small star is in a circular orbit of radius&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi></math>. Then the period of revolution,<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>T</mi></math>&nbsp;depends on&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi></math>&nbsp;as:"}, "options": [{"id": "816bd3", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#8733;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>R</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a3960"}, {"id": "72ad7e", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#8733;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a3961"}, {"id": "d133ed", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#8733;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a3962"}, {"id": "7373c1", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>T</mi><mo mathvariant=\"italic\">&#160;</mo><mo>&#8733;</mo><mi>R</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a3963"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a3964", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb0"], "topics": ["60e5678e499d1e49588f927a"], "concepts": ["6162faf16ed13040ec849361"], "question": {"text": "At a certain depth <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>d</mi></math>&nbsp;below surface of earth,&nbsp;value of acceleration due to gravity becomes four times that of its value at a height <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mi>R</mi></math> above earth surface. Where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi></math> is Radius of earth (Take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>6400</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>km</mi></math>). The depth <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>d</mi></math> is equal to"}, "options": [{"id": "4e92fc", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5260</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>km</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a3965"}, {"id": "2f8285", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>640</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>km</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a3966"}, {"id": "588c37", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2560</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>km</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a3967"}, {"id": "1fe4be", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4800</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>km</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a3968"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a3969", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb0"], "topics": ["60e2fb1f66f9ce3f243b4b6b"], "concepts": ["60e2fb1f66f9ce3f243b4b2f"], "question": {"text": "Escape velocity of a body from earth is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>11</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>2</mn><mo> </mo><mi>km</mi><mo> </mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>. If the radius of a planet be one-third the radius of earth and mass be one-sixth that of earth, the escape velocity from the plate is:"}, "options": [{"id": "502da8", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>11</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>2</mn><mo> </mo><mi>km</mi><mo> </mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a396a"}, {"id": "658a0a", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>8</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>4</mn><mo> </mo><mi>km</mi><mo> </mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a396b"}, {"id": "df8098", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>2</mn><mo> </mo><mi>km</mi><mo> </mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a396c"}, {"id": "20d267", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>7</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>9</mn><mo> </mo><mi>km</mi><mo> </mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a396d"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a396e", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb0"], "topics": ["60e5671c499d1e49588f87fd"], "concepts": ["60e5671a499d1e49588f87c5"], "question": {"text": "A satellite of mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi></math> is in a circular orbit of radius <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></math> about the center of the earth. A meteorite of the same mass, falling towards the earth, collides with the satellite completely inelastic. The speeds of the satellite and the meteorite are the same, just before the collision. The subsequent motion of the combined body will be:"}, "options": [{"id": "423405", "text": "In an elliptical orbit", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a396f"}, {"id": "ecc23b", "text": "Such that it escapes to infinity", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a3970"}, {"id": "5feaee", "text": "In a circular orbit of a different radius", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a3971"}, {"id": "5868c4", "text": "In the same circular orbit of radius <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a3972"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a3973", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb0"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4763"], "concepts": ["60e40fd4f1ef260f84ed1e41"], "question": {"text": "A geostationary satellite is orbiting around an arbitrary planet <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi></math> at a height of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>11</mn><mi>R</mi></math> above the surface of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi></math> being the radius of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi></math>. The time period of another satellite in hours at a height of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi>R</mi></math> from the surface of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi></math> is ________ has the time period of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>24</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>hours</mi><mo>.</mo></math>"}, "options": [{"id": "d4120f", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>6</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a3974"}, {"id": "23c7b4", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>6</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a3975"}, {"id": "2b8e6b", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a3976"}, {"id": "b44fb0", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a3977"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a3978", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb0"], "topics": ["60e2fb5766f9ce3f243b4f3e"], "concepts": ["6162faee6ed13040ec849245"], "question": {"text": "<p>Inside a uniform spherical shell :</p><p>(a) The gravitational field is zero.</p><p>(b) The gravitational potential is zero.</p><p>(c) The gravitational field is the same everywhere.</p><p>(d) The gravitation potential is the same everywhere.</p><p>(e) All the above.</p><p>Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:</p>"}, "options": [{"id": "92c7d6", "text": "(a), (c) and (d) only", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a3979"}, {"id": "60c70a", "text": "(a), (b) and (c) only", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a397a"}, {"id": "9eb304", "text": "(b), (c) and (d) only", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a397b"}, {"id": "79a702", "text": "(e) only", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a397c"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a397d", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb0"], "topics": ["60e5678e499d1e49588f927a"], "concepts": ["60e567fa499d1e49588fa01b"], "question": {"text": "Assume there are two identical simple pendulum Clocks-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn></math> is placed on the earth and Clock-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math> is placed on a space station located at a height h above the earth surface. Clock-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn></math> and Clock-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math> operate at time periods <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>6</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></math> respectively. Then the value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>h</mi></math> is -<br />(consider radius of earth <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>6400</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>km</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>g</mi></math> on earth <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></math>)"}, "options": [{"id": "5d003c", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1200</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>km</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a397e"}, {"id": "edd57a", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1600</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>km</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a397f"}, {"id": "dbf116", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3200</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>km</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a3980"}, {"id": "bad207", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4800</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>km</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a3981"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a3982", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb0"], "topics": ["6117b60a478ae845c43c63f1"], "concepts": ["6117b8bd67dbe2603c3e85a2"], "question": {"text": "A small point of mass $m$ is placed at a distance $2 R$ from the centre ' $O^{\\prime}$ of a big uniform solid sphere of mass M and radius R . The gravitational force on ' m ' due to M is $\\mathrm{F}_1$. A spherical part of radius $\\mathrm{R} / 3$ is removed from the big sphere as shown in the figure and the gravitational force on m due to remaining part of M is found to be $\\mathrm{F}_2$. The value of ratio $\\mathrm{F}_1: \\mathrm{F}_2$ is\n\n<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2FRDgeUFlbNVcGwIS91jCJcAxMlBJ5UuncVuYJJ-tDRVo.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\"/><br/>"}, "options": [{"id": "e2f56b", "text": "$12: 11$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a3983"}, {"id": "df32bc", "text": "$11: 10$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a3984"}, {"id": "a77fbe", "text": "$12: 9$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a3985"}, {"id": "ae0df7", "text": "$16: 9$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a3986"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a3987", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb0"], "topics": ["60e2fb1f66f9ce3f243b4b6b"], "concepts": ["60e2fb9d66f9ce3f243b52bb"], "question": {"text": "<p>Four spheres each of mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>m</mi></math> form <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>a</mi></math> square of side <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>d</mi></math> (as shown in figure). A fifth sphere of mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>M</mi></math> is situated at the centre of square. The total gravitational potential energy of the system is</p><p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2Fb4b27535-c275-4b6f-b8e0-3446f07e8da7-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 150px; height: 165px;\" /></p>"}, "options": [{"id": "f8517a", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>G</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow><mi>d</mi></mfrac><mfenced open=\"[\" close=\"]\"><mrow><mfenced><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow></mfenced><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mi>M</mi></mrow></mfenced></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a3988"}, {"id": "4fd1e6", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>G</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow><mi>d</mi></mfrac><mfenced open=\"[\" close=\"]\"><mrow><mfenced><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow></mfenced><mi>M</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfenced></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a3989"}, {"id": "612fc6", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>G</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow><mi>d</mi></mfrac><mfenced open=\"[\" close=\"]\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mi>M</mi></mrow></mfenced></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a398a"}, {"id": "7cc985", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>G</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow><mi>d</mi></mfrac><mfenced open=\"[\" close=\"]\"><mrow><mn>6</mn><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mi>M</mi></mrow></mfenced></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a398b"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a398c", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb0"], "topics": ["60e5671c499d1e49588f87fd"], "concepts": ["60e56812499d1e49588fa38a"], "question": {"text": "Correct formula for height of a satellite from earths surface is:"}, "options": [{"id": "fb9052", "text": "$\\left(\\frac{T^2 R^2}{4 \\pi^2 g}\\right)^{1 / 3}-R$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a398d"}, {"id": "edaeea", "text": "$\\left(\\frac{T^2 R^2 g}{4 \\pi^2}\\right)^{1 / 3}-R$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a398e"}, {"id": "8dc5d0", "text": "$\\left(\\frac{T^2 R^2 g}{4 \\pi^2}\\right)^{-1 / 3}+R$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a398f"}, {"id": "64e7ed", "text": "$\\left(\\frac{T^2 R^2 g}{4 \\pi}\\right)^{1 / 2}-R$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a3990"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a3991", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb0"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4763"], "concepts": ["60e5671a499d1e49588f87cb"], "question": {"text": "The orbital angular momentum of a satellite is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi></math>, when it is revolving in a circular orbit at height <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></math>&nbsp;from earth surface. If the distance of satellite from the earth centre is increased by eight times to its initial value, then the new angular momentum will be"}, "options": [{"id": "3d4302", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>8</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a3992"}, {"id": "ed1b27", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>9</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a3993"}, {"id": "70449f", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a3994"}, {"id": "809246", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a3995"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a3996", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb0"], "topics": ["60e5671c499d1e49588f87fd"], "concepts": ["60f34752eff4df09e43c25d2"], "question": {"text": "Two satellites, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math> and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></math>, have masses <math><mi>m</mi></math> and <math><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi></math> respectively. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math> is in a circular orbit of radius <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi></math>&nbsp;and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></math> is in a circular orbit of radius <math><mn>2</mn><mi>R</mi></math> around the earth. The ratio of their kinetic energies, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msub><mi>K</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></msub><msub><mi>K</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></msub></mfrac></math> is:"}, "options": [{"id": "4fd7e8", "text": "<math><mn>2</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a3997"}, {"id": "89a5f5", "text": "<math><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a3998"}, {"id": "45b5e6", "text": "<math><mn>1</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a3999"}, {"id": "a00a52", "text": "<math><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></msqrt></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a399a"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a399b", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb0"], "topics": ["60e5678e499d1e49588f927a"], "concepts": ["60e5678c499d1e49588f9251"], "question": {"text": "<p>Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R.</p><p><strong>Assertion A:</strong> If we move from poles to equator, the direction of acceleration due to gravity of earth always points towards the center of earth without any variation in its magnitude.</p><p><strong>Reason R: </strong>At equator, the direction of acceleration due to the gravity is towards the center of earth.</p><p>In the light of above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below</p>"}, "options": [{"id": "8b9d8e", "text": "Assertion and reason both are correct and reason is correct explanation of assertion.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a399c"}, {"id": "1a61ec", "text": "Assertion and reason both are correct but reason is not correct explanation of assertion.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a399d"}, {"id": "d24d4a", "text": "Assertion is true but reason is false", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a399e"}, {"id": "d14c2a", "text": "Assertion is false but reason is true", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a399f"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a39a0", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb0"], "topics": ["60e2fb1f66f9ce3f243b4b6b"], "concepts": ["60e56768499d1e49588f8ed7"], "question": {"text": "A rocket has to be launched from earth in such a way that it never returns. If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>E</mi></math> is the minimum energy delivered by the rocket launcher, what should be the minimum energy that the launcher should have, if the same rocket is to be launched from the surface of the moon? Assume that the density of the earth and the moon are equal and that the earth&#39;s volume is <math><mn>64</mn></math> times the volume of the moon."}, "options": [{"id": "9119fc", "text": "<math><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>64</mn></mrow></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a39a1"}, {"id": "c595c7", "text": "<math><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a39a2"}, {"id": "90339d", "text": "<math><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>32</mn></mrow></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a39a3"}, {"id": "c2fa76", "text": "<math><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>16</mn></mrow></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a39a4"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a39a5", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb0"], "topics": ["6117b60a478ae845c43c63f1"], "concepts": ["6117b607478ae845c43c62fb"], "question": {"text": "A satellite of mass<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>M</mi></math> &nbsp;is launched vertically upwards with an initial speed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>u</mi></math> from the surface of the earth. After it reaches height <math><mi>R</mi></math> ( <math><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo></math> radius of the earth), it ejects a rocket of mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi>M</mi><mn>10</mn></mfrac></math> so that subsequently the satellite moves in a circular orbit. The kinetic energy of the rocket is ( <math><mi>G</mi></math> is the gravitational constant; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></math> is the mass of the earth):"}, "options": [{"id": "4f5e6f", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi>M</mi><mn>20</mn></mfrac><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>113</mn><mn>200</mn></mfrac><mfrac><mrow><mi>G</mi><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow><mi>R</mi></mfrac></mrow></mfenced></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a39a6"}, {"id": "a7fc81", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn><mi>M</mi><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mn>119</mn><mn>200</mn></mfrac><mfrac><mrow><mi>G</mi><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow><mi>R</mi></mfrac></mrow></mfenced></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a39a7"}, {"id": "55a244", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>M</mi></mrow><mn>8</mn></mfrac><msup><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mn>5</mn><mi>G</mi><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>6</mn><mi>R</mi></mrow></mfrac></msqrt></mrow></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a39a8"}, {"id": "40c64e", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi>M</mi><mn>20</mn></mfrac><msup><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>-</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>G</mi><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>R</mi></mrow></mfrac></msqrt></mrow></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a39a9"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a39aa", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb0"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4763"], "concepts": ["60e40fd4f1ef260f84ed1e41"], "question": {"text": "<p>Every planet revolves around the sun in an elliptical orbit :</p><p>A. The force acting on a planet is inversely proportional to square of distance from sun.</p><p>B. Force acting on planet is inversely proportional to product of the masses of the planet and the sun.</p><p>C. The centripetal force acting on the planet is directed away from the sun.</p><p>D. The square of time period of revolution of planet around sun is directly proportional to cube of semi-major axis of elliptical orbit.</p><p>Choose the correct answer from the options given below :</p>"}, "options": [{"id": "8eba76", "text": "A and D only", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a39ab"}, {"id": "3afc17", "text": "C and D only", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a39ac"}, {"id": "7e87a7", "text": "B and C only", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a39ad"}, {"id": "4addcd", "text": "A and C only", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a39ae"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a39af", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb0"], "topics": ["60e2fb1f66f9ce3f243b4b6b"], "concepts": ["60e2fb1f66f9ce3f243b4b2f"], "question": {"text": "Earth has mass 8 times and radius 2 times that of a planet. If the escape velocity from the earth is $11.2 \\mathrm{~km} / \\mathrm{s}$, the escape velocity in $\\mathrm{km} / \\mathrm{s}$ from the planet will be :"}, "options": [{"id": "af783e", "text": "$2.8$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a39b0"}, {"id": "91f3e7", "text": "$11.2$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a39b1"}, {"id": "8a3c98", "text": "$5.6$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a39b2"}, {"id": "62204c", "text": "$8.4$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a39b3"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a39b4", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb0"], "topics": ["60e5678e499d1e49588f927a"], "concepts": ["6162faf16ed13040ec849361"], "question": {"text": "Assuming the earth to be a sphere of uniform mass density, a body weighed $300 \\mathrm{~N}$ on the surface of earth. How much it would weigh at R/4 depth under surface of earth ?"}, "options": [{"id": "ec5ab3", "text": "$75 \\mathrm{~N}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a39b5"}, {"id": "38fb96", "text": "$300 \\mathrm{~N}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a39b6"}, {"id": "069a2e", "text": "$375 \\mathrm{~N}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a39b7"}, {"id": "b4b65f", "text": "$225 \\mathrm{~N}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a39b8"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a39b9", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb0"], "topics": ["60e5671c499d1e49588f87fd"], "concepts": ["60e5671a499d1e49588f87c5"], "question": {"text": "A satellite is moving in a low nearly circular orbit around the earth. Its radius is roughly equal to that of the earth&#39;s radius&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></msub><mo>.</mo></math>&nbsp;By firing rockets attached to it, its speed is instantaneously increased in the direction of its motion so that it become&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msqrt><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msqrt></math> times larger. Due to this the farthest distance from the centre of the earth that the satellite reaches is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></math>. Value of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></math> is :"}, "options": [{"id": "63bd55", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></msub></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a39ba"}, {"id": "feec0f", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></msub></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a39bb"}, {"id": "dae67b", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></msub></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a39bc"}, {"id": "fe66fa", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></msub></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a39bd"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a39be", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb0"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4763"], "concepts": ["60e5671a499d1e49588f87cb"], "question": {"text": "<p>A planet revolving in elliptical orbit has :</p><p>A. a constant velocity of revolution.</p><p>B. has the least velocity when it is nearest to the sun.</p><p>C. its areal velocity is directly proportional to its velocity.</p><p>D. areal velocity is inversely proportional to its velocity.</p><p>E. to follow a trajectory such that the areal velocity is constant.</p><p>Choose the correct answer from the options given below:</p>"}, "options": [{"id": "f7af0b", "text": "A only", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a39bf"}, {"id": "fffd44", "text": "C only", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a39c0"}, {"id": "6aac6b", "text": "E only", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a39c1"}, {"id": "4c738e", "text": "D only", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a39c2"}], "questionLabels": []}], "markingScheme": {"_id": "608283e223cf4b3ad8f125e6", "title": "JEE Single Correct", "correctAttempt": 4, "incorrectAttempt": -1, "createdAt": "2021-04-09T08:45:27.797Z", "updatedAt": "2021-07-05T05:39:15.785Z", "__v": 0, "updated_by": "6042252509c5bf34e8a2f178"}, "maximumAttemptLimit": 20, "linkedQuestions": [], "createdAt": "2026-04-14T14:30:45.832Z", "updatedAt": "2026-04-14T14:30:45.832Z", "__v": 0}, "position": 0, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a3a0b"}, {"sectionId": {"_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a3a09", "title": "Physics Numerical", "sectionType": "normal", "subject": "607018ee404ae53194e73d90", "questions": [{"_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a39ed", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb0"], "topics": ["60e2fb1f66f9ce3f243b4b6b"], "concepts": ["60e56768499d1e49588f8ed7"], "question": {"text": "A satellite of mass 1000 kg is launched to revolve around the earth in an orbit at a height of 270 km from the earth's surface. Kinetic energy of the satellite in this orbit is ______ $\\times 10^{10} \\mathrm{~J}$.<br/>(Mass of earth $=6 \\times 10^{24} \\mathrm{~kg}$, Radius of earth $=$ $6.4 \\times 10^6 \\mathrm{~m}$, Gravitational constant $=$<br/>$\\left.6.67 \\times 10^{-11} \\mathrm{Nm}^2 \\mathrm{~kg}^{-2}\\right)$"}, "options": [{"id": "77a765", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a39ee"}, {"id": "d42c3d", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a39ef"}, {"id": "099116", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a39f0"}, {"id": "ee30f7", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a39f1"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a39f2", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb0"], "topics": ["60e5671c499d1e49588f87fd"], "concepts": ["60e5671a499d1e49588f87c5"], "question": {"text": "<p>Suppose two planets (spherical in shape) of radii <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo></math>&nbsp;but mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>M</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>9</mn><mi>M</mi></math> respectively have a centre to centre separation <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>8</mn><mi>R</mi></math> as shown in the figure. A satellite of mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>m</mi></math>&nbsp;is projected from the surface of the planet of mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>M</mi></math> directly towards the centre of the second planet. The minimum speed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>v</mi></math> required for the satellite to reach the surface of the second planet is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msqrt><mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mn>7</mn></mfrac><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"italic\">G</mi><mi mathvariant=\"italic\">M</mi></mrow><mi mathvariant=\"italic\">R</mi></mfrac></msqrt></math>, then the value of&nbsp; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>a</mi></math>&nbsp;is<br />[Given : The two planets are fixed in their position]</p><p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2F6d6ce075-2e07-4240-8c0d-4d740bb060b9-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 201px; height: 125px;\" /></p>"}, "options": [{"id": "92e4d4", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a39f3"}, {"id": "edaf04", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a39f4"}, {"id": "5b5535", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a39f5"}, {"id": "322ad7", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a39f6"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a39f7", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb0"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4763"], "concepts": ["60e2fad966f9ce3f243b4719"], "question": {"text": "Two satellites revolve around a planet in coplanar circular orbits in anticlockwise direction. Their period of revolutions are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn></math>&nbsp;hour and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>8</mn></math> hours respectively. The radius of the orbit of nearer satellite is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>km</mi><mo>.</mo></math>&nbsp;The angular speed of the farther satellite as observed from the nearer satellite at the instant when both the satellites are closest is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi>&#960;</mi><mi>x</mi></mfrac><mo>rad</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>, where&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi></math> is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>_</mi><mi>_</mi><mi>_</mi><mi>_</mi><mi>_</mi><mi>_</mi><mi>_</mi><mo>.</mo></math>"}, "options": [{"id": "33a271", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a39f8"}, {"id": "27fe33", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a39f9"}, {"id": "7e656a", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a39fa"}, {"id": "365ef9", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a39fb"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a39fc", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb0"], "topics": ["60e2fb1f66f9ce3f243b4b6b"], "concepts": ["60e2fb9d66f9ce3f243b52bb"], "question": {"text": "An asteroid is moving directly towards the centre&nbsp;of the earth. When at a distance of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn><mi>R</mi></math> (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi></math> is the radius of the earth) from the centre of the earth, it has a speed of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>12</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>km</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>.</mo></math> Neglecting the effect of earth&#39;s atmosphere, what will be the speed of the asteroid when it hits the surface of the earth (escape velocity from the earth is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>11.2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></math> ? Give your answer to the nearest integer in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>km</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>__________."}, "options": [{"id": "fde96a", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a39fd"}, {"id": "c1a856", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a39fe"}, {"id": "92668e", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a39ff"}, {"id": "91f4bb", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a3a00"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a3a01", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb0"], "topics": ["60e2fb1f66f9ce3f243b4b6b"], "concepts": ["60e2fb9d66f9ce3f243b52bb"], "question": {"text": "<p>If one wants to remove all the mass of the earth to infinity in order to break it up completely. The amount of energy that needs to be supplied will be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi>x</mi><mn>5</mn></mfrac><mfrac><mrow><mi>G</mi><msup><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mi>R</mi></mfrac></math> where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi></math> is ________.</p><p>(Round off to the Nearest Integer)</p><p>(<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>M</mi></math>&nbsp;is the mass of earth, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi></math> is the radius of earth,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>G</mi></math> is the gravitational constant)</p>"}, "options": [{"id": "3279f3", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a3a02"}, {"id": "86bb37", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a3a03"}, {"id": "d23aa6", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a3a04"}, {"id": "1c1180", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a3a05"}], "questionLabels": []}], "markingScheme": {"_id": "608283c623cf4b3ad8f125e5", "title": "JEE Numerical", "correctAttempt": 4, "incorrectAttempt": -1, "createdAt": "2021-04-09T08:45:27.797Z", "updatedAt": "2021-07-05T05:39:27.056Z", "__v": 0, "updated_by": "6042252509c5bf34e8a2f178"}, "maximumAttemptLimit": 5, "linkedQuestions": [], "createdAt": "2026-04-14T14:30:45.928Z", "updatedAt": "2026-04-14T14:30:45.928Z", "__v": 0}, "position": 1, "_id": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a3a0c"}], "bonusSections": [], "packDetails": [], "paidFeatures": [], "tags": [], "timeRestrictionSlots": [], "createdAt": "2026-04-14T11:15:55.422Z", "updatedAt": "2026-04-17T04:57:58.675Z", "testType": "PYQs as Chapter Tests", "isResumeBufferAllowed": true, "enableAccessibility": true, "maxAttemptableQuestion": 25, "analysisVideoThumbnail": "", "analysisVideoThumbnailDark": "", "shortTitle": "", "subjectDetails": [{"subjectId": "607018ee404ae53194e73d90", "title": "Physics", "shortName": "Phy"}], "testTimerType": "mm", "isMarkForReviewAvailable": true}, "temporaryTestDetail": {"_id": "6a4a1e2b9cbf3c65130394a5", "testId": "69de21eb7e39d99b57bc5aaf", "userId": "6914cc9c4d7566258ee3a8fd", "startedAt": "2026-07-05T09:04:43.096Z", "lastAttemptedSection": null, "totalTimeLeft": 50, "bonusAttempt": false, "sections": [{"sectionId": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a39c7", "questions": [{"questionId": "69de4f952ee0e063d92a395f", "markedOptions": [], "inputValue": null, "timeTaken": 0, "timeLeftWhenAttempted": null, "status": "notVisited", "_id": "6a4a1e2b9cbf3c65130394a7",